  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the locker room, Fedya checks on Senya who is feeling very sick. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не ешь эту рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты уголь пил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ага, две пачки сожрал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ни хрена себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня болит живот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе надо пойти домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу работать сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Иди домой и отдыхай.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around Bad Fish line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you all right?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I am very bad.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'very much' directly with 'badly', combining 'very much' with 'badly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I told you.' The verb for 'said' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не ешь эту рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not eat that fish.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'eat' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I thought she was normal.' Senya chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'thought' for the ongoing action, followed by 'normal'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты уголь пил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you drink charcoal?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'was drinking' is enough to make it a question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ага, две пачки сожрал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yeah, I ate two packs.' The phrasing opens with 'yeah' before bringing in 'two' and 'pack' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ни хрена себе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No shit.' Fedya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'horseradish'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня болит живот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My stomach hurts.' Senya uses the classic Russian possession phrasing — literally 'at me' rather than a verb meaning 'to have', combining 'hurts' with 'belly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе надо пойти домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You need to go home.' Russian builds this impersonally with the dative ('to me' or 'to you') and 'home'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу работать сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I cannot work today.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'can' to negate the action. Then Fedya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Иди домой и отдыхай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go home and rest.' Fedya leads with the direct command form meaning 'go', pairing it with 'home' and 'rest'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "У тебя все хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.5
